-- title:
--   An element lies in $\operatorname{Her}(h)$ as soon as both $zz^*$ and $z^*z$ do
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a C*-algebra (not necessarily unital) with its usual order. For $h\in A$ let $\operatorname{Her}(h)=\overline{hAh^*}$ be the norm closure of $\{hah^*: a\in A\}$; it is a closed $*$-subalgebra of $A$ (for positive $h$, the hereditary C*-subalgebra generated by $h$).
--
--   **Theorem.** For all $h,z\in A$,
--
--   $$
--   zz^*\in\operatorname{Her}(h)\quad\text{and}\quad z^*z\in\operatorname{Her}(h)\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad z\in\operatorname{Her}(h).
--   $$
--
--   So membership of an element in such a subalgebra is controlled by its two moduli $zz^*$ and $z^*z$. A typical use: if $S$ is an isometric map with $S(e)^*S(e)=e^*e$ for $e$ in a hereditary subalgebra and $S(e)S(e)^*$ lands in another one, both contained in $\operatorname{Her}(k)$, then $S(e)$ itself belongs to $\operatorname{Her}(k)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** In Lean the order on $A$ is an arbitrary partial order making $A$ a star-ordered ring; in a C*-algebra this is necessarily the usual order, whose positive cone consists of the elements $s^*s$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/CStarAlgebra/PureInfiniteness/Cutoffs.lean#L46-L76

